```powershell
# 1. CONFIGURATION
# Replace these with your values:
$resourceGroup         = "myResourceGroup"
$automationAccountName = "myAutomationAccount"
$runbookName           = "MyRunbook"
# If your runbook takes parameters, define a hashtable here:
# e.g. $runbookParams = @{ "VMName" = "testvm"; "Action" = "Start" }
$runbookParams         = @{}

# 2. (Optional) login â if you already have 'az login' done, skip this
Write-Host "Logging in to Azure..."
az account show > $null 2>&1
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {
    az login | Out-Null
}

# 3. START THE RUNBOOK
Write-Host "Starting runbook '$runbookName' in Automation Account '$automationAccountName'..."
# Build the --parameters string if you have any
if ($runbookParams.Count -gt 0) {
    $paramArgs = $runbookParams.GetEnumerator() |
                 ForEach-Object { "$($_.Key)=$($_.Value)" } |
                 Join-String " "
} else {
    $paramArgs = ""
}

# Start the runbook, capture the jobId
$jobId = az automation runbook start `
    --resource-group      $resourceGroup `
    --automation-account  $automationAccountName `
    --name                $runbookName `
    --parameters          $paramArgs `
    --query               "jobId" `
    -o tsv

if (-not $jobId) {
    Write-Error "Failed to start runbook."
    exit 1
}
Write-Host "Runbook started. Job ID: $jobId"

# 4. WAIT FOR JOB TO COMPLETE
Write-Host "Waiting for job to finish..."
az automation job wait `
    --resource-group     $resourceGroup `
    --automation-account $automationAccountName `
    --job-id             $jobId `
    --timeout            600 `
    --interval           5

if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {
    Write-Warning "Job wait returned non-zero exit code; it may have timed out or failed."
}

# 5. FETCH JOB OUTPUT STREAMS
Write-Host "Retrieving job outputs (Output, Warning, Error streams)..."
# This returns an array of objects with 'streamType' & 'streamText'
$outputs = az automation job output list `
    --resource-group     $resourceGroup `
    --automation-account $automationAccountName `
    --job-id             $jobId `
    --query              "[].{Type:streamType,Text:streamText}" `
    -o json | ConvertFrom-Json

if (-not $outputs) {
    Write-Warning "No output streams were produced."
} else {
    foreach ($line in $outputs) {
        switch ($line.Type) {
            "Error"   { Write-Error   $line.Text; break }
            "Warning" { Write-Warning $line.Text; break }
            default   { Write-Host    $line.Text; break }
        }
    }
}

# 6. (Optional) GET FINAL JOB STATUS
$finalStatus = az automation job show `
    --resource-group     $resourceGroup `
    --automation-account $automationAccountName `
    --job-id             $jobId `
    --query              "status" `
    -o tsv

Write-Host "Job $jobId finished with status: $finalStatus"
```

How it works, step by step:

1. You set your Azure resourceâgroup, Automation account name, runbook name and any parameters.
2. Optionally do `az login` if you arenât already authenticated.
3. Call `az automation runbook start â¦` and capture the returned `jobId`.
4. Use `az automation job wait â¦` to block until the job completes (or times out).
5. Once done, call `az automation job output list â¦` to pull back every stream entry (Output, Warning, Error).
6. Loop through the streams and write them to the console with the appropriate severity.
7. Finally, you can also query the jobâs final status (`Completed`, `Failed`, etc.).

This gives you a simple, endâtoâend script that starts a runbook and prints out whatever it logged.
